7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:45,250
Story of the Week.

8
00:00:45,250 --> 00:00:51,950
Claude Designs Proteins Faster and More Accurately Than Human Researchers – Completely Unsupervised.

9
00:00:51,950 --> 00:01:11,487
14 of 15 target proteins successfully designed, without a single human intervention during the process: Anthropic had Claude Opus 4.8 and the new Mythos Preview run a multi-day wet lab campaign autonomously – and the results significantly exceed what the biotech industry has been used to so far .

10
00:01:11,487 --> 00:01:25,275
In collaboration with the independent labs Adaptyv Bio and Twist Bioscience, the models tested 1,320 designs against 15 target proteins, validated in the real lab rather than just in simulation .

11
00:01:25,275 --> 00:01:38,687
Mythos Preview achieved a 26.7% hit rate over 48 hours in multi-target mode, Opus 4.8 reached 22.6% – the industry average is only 10-15% .

12
00:01:38,687 --> 00:01:45,737
In single-target mode over 24 hours, Mythos Preview even climbed to 35.1% .

13
00:01:45,737 --> 00:02:03,275
The most striking case is the extreme outlier target protein RBX1: there, Mythos Preview achieved a 40% hit rate – in an open Adaptyv Bio competition with 245 human participants, the best submissions reached just 3.7% .

14
00:02:03,275 --> 00:02:09,662
In total, 354 of 1,320 tested designs were confirmed in the lab .

15
00:02:09,662 --> 00:02:19,850
Anthropic emphasizes that the models carried out design, iteration, and evaluation entirely on their own – no human had to intervene between iteration steps .

16
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:29,012
Direct quotes from the technical preprint are not yet publicly accessible, as the publication is either paywalled or only available as a preprint .

17
00:02:29,012 --> 00:02:30,212
Why does this matter?

18
00:02:30,212 --> 00:02:44,612
For biotech startups and pharmaceutical companies, this means: design campaigns that used to tie up entire research teams for months can now be run in days with a single model setup – at a hit rate that beats specialized human teams.

19
00:02:44,612 --> 00:02:51,862
This shifts the bottleneck in drug and materials development from the design phase to lab validation and production scaling.

20
00:02:51,862 --> 00:03:02,725
Critical voices: Open questions remain: How well do these results generalize to more complex, therapeutically relevant target proteins beyond the 15 test cases?

21
00:03:02,725 --> 00:03:12,750
And how reproducible are the results outside the tightly curated partnership with Adaptyv Bio and Twist Bioscience, whose validation processes Anthropic helped shape?

22
00:03:12,750 --> 00:03:24,412
Bottom Line: Claude has shown that autonomous, multi-day scientific design campaigns with above-industry success rates are possible – the next question is whether this also scales to harder targets.

23
00:03:24,412 --> 00:03:25,750
More Top Stories.

24
00:03:25,750 --> 00:03:29,775
Stripe Acquires OpenRouter for Over $7B.

25
00:03:29,775 --> 00:03:41,725
Stripe is buying the AI model routing startup OpenRouter, which serves as a central access point to over 400 AI models from more than 80 providers and protects customers from vendor lock-in .

26
00:03:41,725 --> 00:04:01,612
The purchase price of over $7B is 5.4x the Series B valuation of $1.3B that OpenRouter achieved just in May 2026 in a $113M funding round with investors including Sequoia, Andreessen Horowitz, Menlo Ventures, and Alphabet's CapitalG .

27
00:04:01,612 --> 00:04:11,862
OpenRouter's CEO described the company as "Stripe for AI" – a unified access point for different AI systems, similar to how Stripe unifies payment providers .

28
00:04:11,862 --> 00:04:22,250
With 8 million global users, OpenRouter has become a central infrastructure layer for developers who want to switch between models without rebuilding their integration each time .

29
00:04:22,250 --> 00:04:23,075
So what?

30
00:04:23,075 --> 00:04:38,475
For teams already testing multiple models through OpenRouter today, this is a signal: payment infrastructure and AI infrastructure are converging – anyone who wants billing and model routing from a single provider should keep an eye on the Stripe integration path.

31
00:04:38,475 --> 00:04:44,512
US Pressures 35 Countries to Choose Between US and China AI Ecosystems.

32
00:04:44,618 --> 00:04:56,293
The US State Department is preparing a letter to 35 signatory states of the "Pax Silica" declaration (AI Opportunity Statement) demanding a clear commitment to one technology camp .

33
00:04:56,293 --> 00:05:07,118
"Signature of the Pax Silica Declaration is not merely a membership subscription, but a commitment"  – the wording makes clear that Washington no longer tolerates dual membership.

34
00:05:07,118 --> 00:05:15,005
China counters with its own "World Artificial Intelligence Cooperation Organization" and promotes open models as an alternative .

35
00:05:15,005 --> 00:05:22,743
Kazakhstan is so far the only country to have joined both blocs, which has raised concerns in Washington about dual loyalty .

36
00:05:22,743 --> 00:05:29,130
According to Reuters reporting, the letter has not yet been officially sent, and the draft could still change .

37
00:05:29,130 --> 00:05:29,955
So what?

38
00:05:29,955 --> 00:05:46,443
For internationally active AI companies, geopolitics is becoming a compliance issue: anyone with customers or data centers in multiple of the 35 countries should actively track the bloc formation before export controls or procurement rules get tied to it.

39
00:05:46,443 --> 00:05:51,430
Nvidia Customers Warned of AI Chip Price Hikes Above 15%.

40
00:05:51,430 --> 00:06:02,855
Server manufacturers building systems with Nvidia's Vera Rubin and Grace Blackwell chips for Microsoft, Google, and Oracle have been informed of price increases above 15% .

41
00:06:02,855 --> 00:06:14,418
Rising memory chip costs are the cause; the increases affect systems shipping from early 2027, with the exact amount varying by chip generation and memory configuration .

42
00:06:14,418 --> 00:06:21,130
Direct quotes from manufacturers are not available per Bloomberg's reporting, which relies on sources at suppliers .

43
00:06:21,130 --> 00:06:21,955
So what?

44
00:06:21,955 --> 00:06:34,605
Anyone doing 2027 capacity planning for GPU clusters should factor the price surcharge into budgets now – AI infrastructure costs keep rising, independent of the model pricing competition.

